{{Culture of Balisca}}

In [[Balisca]], a '''skyscraper revolt''' ({{Langx|bl|Revolta vertical|4=vertical revolt}}) is a organized protest against the planning or construction of supertall and megatall skyscrapers in the [[Baliscan Confederation]]. The revolts first emerged in the 1970s and 1980s in response to the relaxation of post-earthquake height restrictions and the subsequent boom in high-rise construction, and gained national momentum in the 1990s and 2000s as supertall towers became increasingly common. The movement reached its watershed with the [[Ampurias Tower]] controversy (2008–2021), which propelled anti-skyscraper activism from a local concern into a confederal political movement.

Unlike the international [[W:Highway revolts|highway revolts]], which were primarily motivated by neighborhood [[W:Displacement|displacement]] and the [[W:Car dependency|prioritization of automobile traffic]] — the Baliscan skyscraper revolt was driven by a distinctive combination of ecological, democratic, and cooperative concerns: the disruption of urban wind corridors and air circulation (the ''[[efecto muro]]'', or "wall effect"), [[W:Light pollution|light pollution]], the concentration of commercial space in speculative towers while the confederation faced a chronic [[Baliscan housing shortage|housing shortage]], and the perception that megatall architecture represented a form of [[W:Wealth concentration|wealth concentration]] and widening [[W:Economic inequality|economic inequality]].

In the thirteen years since the first supertall tower was completed in 1993, 50,479 of Balisca's 57,681 [[Communes of Balisca|communes]] have either introduced restrictions on supertalls or banned them entirely. Most notably, the [[Castejón Metropolis]] introduced a ban on the construction of buildings over {{Convert|400|m|ft}} in 2024, following continued concerns regarding low office occupancy rates and the controversy surrounding the megatall [[Ampurias Tower]]. The revolt's legacy is the [[Casajuan Plan]] — the confederal urban redevelopment programme that redirected construction from speculative commercial towers toward cooperative housing, adaptive reuse, and mid-rise "[[W:Plyscraper|plyscraper]]" development using [[W:Mass timber|mass timber]].

