== Castejón Metropolis ==

{{main|Ampurias Tower|Casajuan Plan|2021 Baliscan civil unrest}}

The Castejón Metropolis is the epicenter of the skyscraper revolt — the site of its most dramatic confrontations, its most consequential political battles, and its most significant legislative outcome.

=== Early verticalization (1950s–1980s) ===

From the 1950s onward, Castejón experienced a period of intense verticalization. The capital's 50-metre height restriction, imposed after the [[1924 Alpujarra earthquake]], was progressively relaxed as the [[Baliscan miracle|post-Great Adonian War economy]] expanded and the confederal government sought to accommodate the growing administrative and commercial functions of the capital. The first wave of high-rise construction — towers of 20 to 30 stories — was broadly accepted as a necessary response to urban growth, though the earliest objections to the ''efecto muro'' date from this period. A 1972 study by the [[Castejón Institute of Urban Ecology]] documented measurable reductions in air circulation along the [[Salto Bay]] waterfront corridor, where a row of new towers had created a wind shadow that trapped vehicle exhaust and particulate matter against the ground. The study coined the term ''efecto muro'' and recommended that new construction along the waterfront be limited to 15 stories — a recommendation that the Metropolitan Council ignored.

The first significant anti-skyscraper protest in Castejón occurred in 1978, when residents of the [[La Plata]] barrio occupied the construction site of the 28-story [[Torre del Mar]], a mixed-use tower proposed for a lot adjacent to the historic [[Mercadero]]. The occupation — organised by the [[Communal Assembly of La Plata|Assembly of La Plata]] and supported by the [[Ecological Action]] platform — lasted eleven days and ended when the communal council agreed to reduce the tower's height to 20 stories and incorporate a public market on the ground floor. The La Plata occupation established the template for subsequent revolts: communal assembly organization, ecological and democratic framing, physical occupation of the construction site, and negotiation toward a compromise that incorporated public benefit.

=== The supertall era (1993–2008) ===

The completion of [[333 Trovadores]] in 1993 — a 78-storey, 318-metre tower in the [[Expansión]] borough — marked the beginning of the supertall era in Castejón. The tower was the first building in Balisca to exceed 300 meters, and its construction was supported by the [[Confederal Investment Authority]] as a symbol of Balisca's economic modernization. Opposition was limited but vocal: the [[Concordia School of Architecture]] published a critique arguing that the tower's sheer facade would create a severe ''efecto muro'' along the [[Sant Dídac]] corridor, and the [[Desconexión]] counterculture produced a series of détourned posters depicting the tower as a middle finger raised against the city. The tower was completed without significant disruption, but the critique established the intellectual framework that would later animate the Ampurias Tower revolt.

The following decade saw a cascade of supertall proposals across Castejón: the Torre Atlàntica (92 storeys, approved 1998, completed 2003), the '''Torre del Nord''' (84 storeys, approved 2001, completed 2006), and the '''Torre tba''' (71 storeys, approved 2004, completed 2009). Each provoked local opposition, but the protests remained fragmented — each district fighting on their own without coordination. The [[Coordinadora Anti-Torre]], an umbrella organisation linking communal assemblies across the metropolis, was established in 2005 to coordinate opposition, but it remained a small network with limited political influence.

=== Ampurias Tower and the Siege of Ampurias (2008–2018) ===

The announcement of the Ampurias Tower in 2008 — a 128-storey, 625-meter megatall proposed by the [[Salto Bay Authority]] — transformed the fragmented anti-tower movement into a national political force. The tower was conceived during the tenure of [[Cristiano Sandoval]] as a symbol of Balisca's "re-engagement" with the [[World economy|global economy]], and it was designed by the architects [[Lamar Torres]] and [[Santi Jacintha]] to be "energy positive," incorporating 270 vertical-axis wind turbines into its facade. The ecological framing of the design was intended to defuse the environmental objections that had dogged previous towers; instead, it inflamed them. Critics argued that the turbines were greenwash — that a 625-metre tower's embodied carbon, construction impact, and ecological footprint far outweighed any energy generated by its facade.
[[File:Ampurias Tower (cropped).png|thumb|388x388px|The completed [[Ampurias Tower]] from the [[Tistaina Canal]]]]
The announcement triggered immediate and militant backlash. Opposition was spearheaded by the radical environmentalist front [[Ecological Action]] and cells of the [[Desconexión]] subculture, who cited the Baliscan legal principle of [[bien fortificado|''bien fortificado'']] ("battle-ready democracy") to justify physical resistance against the project. Protests rapidly escalated from civil disobedience to illegalism and sabotage. In what became known as the [[Siege of Ampurias]] in 2011, masked ''desconexiónistas'' and anarchist affinity groups breached the construction perimeter, disabling heavy machinery and establishing a [[W:Zone à défendre|''zone to defend'']] (ZAD) on the foundation site. They engaged in [[W:Détournement|''détournement'']] tactics, hijacking the project's billboards to display anti-consumerist slogans and jamming the Salto Bay Authority's digital communications.

The conflict resulted in violent clashes with the [[Mozos de Escuadra]] and private security contractors. Construction was delayed for nearly two years due to persistent vandal attacks on supply trucks and the "concrete sabotage" campaign, in which protesters mixed sugar into cement shipments to ruin foundation pours. The Salto Bay Authority ultimately negotiated a "Peace Accord" with the activist coalitions, which included the binding agreement to convert the building's base into a public commons and to hand over operational management of the wind turbines to a [[W:Utility cooperative|local cooperative]].

The building was completed on 8 June 2018 and opened to the public on 7 May 2019. Upon opening, it struggled to reach full occupancy. While [[Sifr]], [[Através]], and several major state-owned enterprises moved in, the upper floors — intended for foreign diplomatic missions and eco-tourism hotels — remained largely vacant, due to the cooling global economy and Balisca's restrictive business regulations.

=== ''Baliscazo'' and the vertical commune (2021–2024) ===

The outbreak of the [[COVID-19 pandemic]] in 2020 left the tower nearly empty as Baliscan society rapidly shifted towards remote work. The subsequent [[2021 Baliscan civil unrest|Baliscazo]] brought the building back into the spotlight. During the July protests, demonstrators occupied the lobby and ransacked many of the lower floors, while simultaneously demanding the utilization of the empty space to address the city's critical housing shortage. The occupation of the Ampurias Tower lobby — in which hundreds of protesters established a communal kitchen, a medical station, and a popular assembly on the ground floor — became one of the iconic images of the Baliscazo.

In response, the new administration of [[Zavier Bilal]] and the [[Secretariat of Local Administration and Modernization (Balisca)|Secretariat of Local Administration]] designated the Ampurias Tower as a pilot project for the [[Casajuan Plan]]. Between 2022 and 2024, floors 40 through 110 were retrofitted and converted from commercial office space into limited-equity housing cooperatives. The tower now functions as a "vertical commune," housing over 8,000 residents. The successful conversion has been cited by the Secretariat as a model for sustainable density and the adaptive reuse of infrastructure.

In December 2024, the [[Metropolitan Council of Castejón]] voted 347–52 to ban the construction of buildings exceeding 400 metres within the metropolitan boundary. The ban was the most significant legislative outcome of the revolt in Castejón, and it was widely interpreted as the metropolis's definitive verdict on the Ampurias Tower era.

