== Outcomes and legacy ==

=== The commune ban cascade ===

The Ampurias Tower controversy triggered a cascade of supertall restrictions and bans across the confederation. In the seven years following the tower's completion in 2018, 50,479 of Balisca's 57,681 (87%) communes introduced some form of restriction on supertall construction. The restrictions range from absolute bans (common in smaller communes and outside of the [[Levante, Balisca|Levante]]) to height caps of 200–400 metres (common in Levantine communes) to conditional approval regimes that require environmental impact assessments, wind corridor studies, and cooperative housing set-asides.

=== Casajuan Plan ===

{{main|Casajuan Plan}}

The [[Casajuan Plan]], adopted in 2019, was the revolt's most significant legislative legacy. The plan redirected confederal construction policy away from speculative commercial towers and toward [[W:Adaptive reuse|adaptive reuse]], cooperative-social housing, and mid-rise development. Its key provisions — mandatory conversion of vacant commercial space, 85% affordable housing requirements, net ecological gain standards, and the "no-displacement principle" — directly addressed the grievances that had animated the revolt. The plan's emphasis on mass timber "plyscrapers" — mid-rise buildings of 10 to 20 storeys constructed from engineered wood — offered a concrete alternative to the steel-and-glass supertall, and the first plyscraper projects in Castejón and Concordia were widely celebrated as the architectural expression of the post-revolt settlement.

=== International influence ===

The Baliscan skyscraper revolt has been studied and cited by [[W:Anti-capitalism|anti-capitalist]], anti-skyscraper movements in [[Asikyira]], [[Volisania]], [[Burawa]], and [[Okaiken]], where concerns about the ecological and social impacts of vertical urbanization along wide the economic connotations of skyscrapers proliferation have grown in parallel. 

